# DEBT-0004 — Constitutional corpus not yet populated; clause-id scheme not yet ratified

Status: **OPEN (founder-scoped at B0.3)** · Filed: 2026-07-27 · Channel: CER-3 · Priority: **MEDIUM-HIGH (load-bearing for genesis)**

## Compromise

B0.3 delivers the **Constitution accessor** (`kernel/constitution.py`: read-only
`get_clause`/`query`/`lineage`, no write method — FR-1). It is proven against an in-repo
fixture. **The real Z0 corpus is not populated**: `constitution/` holds only `README.md`,
and no canonical **clause-id scheme** has been ratified for the constitutional documents.

## Reason

Choosing *which documents are the constitution* (`constitution/PAEOS-0..6.md`) and *how
clauses are identified/tagged* is a constitutional-content act, not a Builder act. Under
CER-5 only the founder legislates constitutional content. At B0.3 the founder explicitly
scoped the task to the accessor only and deferred population (see review). Recording the
compromise here per CER-3 rather than having the runtime author the constitution.

## Ideal solution

A founder-legislated **genesis act** that:
1. Fixes the canon — which of `derivation/PAEOS-0..3.5`, `spec/PAEOS-4-v1.1`, and the
   PAEOS-5/6 material become `constitution/PAEOS-0..6.md` (and at which versions).
2. Ratifies the **clause-id scheme**. The accessor currently parses `[cN]` markers with id
   `"<document>#c<n>"` (mirrors SC-05 clause tags and the ClauseRef `"<parent>#c<n>"` shape);
   this is provisional and must be confirmed or replaced, because clause ids are a one-way
   door — SC-05 `ClauseRef`/`serving_clause` reference them everywhere.
3. Tags the corpus and freezes it, so PAEOS-4 §13.1 `constitutional_basis:[HASH]` can pin
   real hashes at genesis (S0).

## Repayment conditions

**Before the vertical slice (B0.SLICE) and before genesis (S0).** The immediate next tasks
are storage-agnostic of constitution *content*: B0.4 (core types) and B0.5 (lifecycle table)
depend on B0.4, not on Z0; B0.6 (gates) uses the *accessor* (which works against an empty
corpus). Real end-to-end gate/seal behaviour and genesis both need the populated corpus.

## Priority

MEDIUM-HIGH. Non-blocking for B0.4–B0.6 as code, but load-bearing for genesis and the slice;
should be scheduled before Phase-0 close-out.

## Estimated impact

Low implementation risk (the accessor already serves whatever Z0 contains), but high
*constitutional* significance: the clause-id scheme, once referenced by policies and goals,
is expensive to change. Front-loading the founder decision here avoids a churn later.

